WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.528
Secure development starts with requirements and design, not just testing at the end.

2
00:00:05.528 --> 00:00:12.757
Threat modeling helps teams reason about what can go wrong before implementation choices become expensive to change.

3
00:00:12.757 --> 00:00:26.789
Abuse-case thinking asks how a feature could be misused: can a user skip a step, change an object ID, upload an unexpected file, replay a request, or trigger a workflow out of order?

4
00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:30.191
These questions turn security into concrete engineering constraints.

5
00:00:30.191 --> 00:00:33.593
Different testing techniques reveal different issues.

6
00:00:33.593 --> 00:00:40.396
Secure code review can find logic flaws, missing authorization checks, unsafe API usage, and weak assumptions.

7
00:00:40.396 --> 00:00:46.775
SAST reviews source or compiled code without running the application and can scale across repositories.

8
00:00:46.775 --> 00:00:54.004
DAST tests a running application from the outside and can reveal behavior that only appears in deployment.

9
00:00:54.004 --> 00:00:57.405
Dependency scanning looks for vulnerable components.

10
00:00:57.405 --> 00:01:02.083
Manual testing ties findings back to business logic, roles, and context.

11
00:01:02.083 --> 00:01:05.485
None of these techniques replaces the others.

12
00:01:05.485 --> 00:01:08.887
Fixing root causes matters more than treating symptoms.

13
00:01:08.887 --> 00:01:17.816
If many endpoints miss authorization checks, the durable fix may be a shared policy pattern and tests, not one patched route.

14
00:01:17.816 --> 00:01:24.195
If input validation differs across controllers, the better answer may be a common validation layer.

15
00:01:24.195 --> 00:01:30.148
If dependency updates repeatedly break builds, the process may need ownership and upgrade windows.

16
00:01:30.148 --> 00:01:39.928
Mature teams document security requirements, add regression tests for fixed issues, monitor production behavior, and feed lessons learned back into design and development.
